payload="\"Reflection Subwords, Noncrossing Combinatorics, and Braids (https://personal.utdallas.edu/~nxw170830/docs/ReflectionSubwords2026/index.html)\\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Source: thm:hsop-hilbert-series; eq:ratcat; Gordon–Griffeth, Theorem 2.11 and §2.18, as cited in the manuscript. ... Source: Stack–Williams, Proposition1.5, Theorem1.8 and Corollary1.9, as recalled in thm:parking-permutation-range and eq:parking-degree-test of the current paper.\\n# 2.Spaces and traces\\n\\nRational Catalan numbers show up as graded dimensions of certain algebraic spaces, and as Hecke traces of certain braids.\\n\\n03:05 · 4 seconds\\n\\nBegin with algebraic parking spaces and their two Hecke trace formulas.\\n\\n2. Spaces and traces 07 / 38\\n\\n## \\\\(\\\\operatorname{Cat}_p(W;q)\\\\) via algebraic parking spaces\\n\\nFix \\\\(p>0\\\\) coprime to \\\\(h\\\\), with a degree-\\\\(p\\\\) hsop \\\\(\\\\Theta_p\\\\cong(V^*)^{\\\\sigma_p}\\\\), and set\\n\\n\\\\[A_{\\\\Theta_p}\\\\defeq\\\\operatorname{Sym}(V^*)/(\\\\Theta_p).\\\\]\\n\\nTheorem (Bessis–Reiner; Gordon–Griffeth)\\n\\nFor \\\\(W\\\\) well-generated, such hsops exist, and\\n\\n\\\\[\\\\begin{aligned} \\\\operatorname{Hilb}(A_{\\\\Theta_p};q)&=\\\\operatorname{Park}_p(W;q),\\\\\\\\ \\\\operatorname{Hilb}(A_{\\\\Theta_p}^{W};q)&=\\\\operatorname{Cat}_p(W;q). \\\\end{aligned}\\\\]\\n\\nProblem: find a finite \\\\(W\\\\)-set whose permutation module is the ungraded \\\\(A_{\\\\Theta_p}\\\\).\\n\\n03:09 · 39 seconds\\n\\nGordon–Griffeth construct the required hsops in every irreducible well-generated group and every positive coprime parameter. The quotient has dimension p^r; its invariants have dimension the rational Catalan number. The generator representation is the Galois twist of V*. Here [p]_q=1+q+...+q^{p-1}.\\n\\nWe have not yet imposed the condition that the Galois twist is isomorphic to V. Until that condition appears, do not assert that this is a permutation representation with character p^{dim V^g}.\\n\\nSource: thm:hsop-hilbert-series; eq:ratcat; Gordon–Griffeth, Theorem 2.11 and §2.18, as cited in the manuscript.\\n\\nThe displayed graded invariants formula is cor:hsop-graded-multiplicities with the trivial character, rather than only its specialization to dimension. The problem concerns the ungraded representation.\\n\\n## When should combinatorics exist?\\n\\nQuestion\\n\\nWhen is an algebraic parking space a permutation module?\\n\\nAnswer\\n\\n\\\\[V^{\\\\sigma_p}\\\\cong V\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad c^p\\\\sim_Wc.\\\\]\\n\\nEvery coprime \\\\(p\\\\) in Weyl type; for \\\\(G(m,1,r)\\\\), \\\\(p\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod m\\\\).\\n\\nFor an irreducible well-generated complex reflection group \\\\(W\\\\) and \\\\(p>0\\\\) coprime to \\\\(h\\\\), the following are equivalent:\\n\\n  * \\\\(c^p\\\\sim_Wc\\\\).\\n  * \\\\(V^{\\\\sigma_p}\\\\cong V\\\\).\\n  * \\\\(\\\\sigma_p\\\\) fixes the reflection field \\\\(\\\\mathbb Q(\\\\operatorname{tr}(w|V):w\\\\in W)\\\\) pointwise.\\n  * A degree-\\\\(p\\\\) hsop carrying \\\\(V^*\\\\) exists.\\n  * \\\\(A_{\\\\Theta_p}\\\\) is an ungraded permutation module.\\n  * \\\\(\\\\{p(d_i-1)\\\\bmod h\\\\}_{i=1}^r=\\\\{d_i-1\\\\}_{i=1}^r\\\\), as multisets.\\n\\nPosh parking spaces characterizes when \\\\(A_{\\\\Theta_p}\\\\) is a permutation module, building on Armstrong–Reiner–Rhoades and Ito–Okada.\\n\\n03:48 · 49 seconds\\n\\nThe standing assumption gcd(p,h)=1 remains in force. We use \\\\(V^{\\\\sigma_p}\\\\cong V\\\\), equivalently \\\\(c^p\\\\sim_Wc\\\\). For an expert, one can also test it by multiplication of the reflection exponents modulo h. The character of the hsop quotient then reduces to p^{dim V^g}.\\n\\nFor Weyl groups V is defined over Q, so every coprime parameter satisfies \\\\(V^{\\\\sigma_p}\\\\cong V\\\\). For G(m,1,r), and for G(m,m,r) with r>=3, the additional congruence is p=1 modulo m. We need precisely this extra condition to find a pure correction to the chosen c.\\n\\nSource: eq:condition; thm:parking-permutation-range. Do not extend the G(m,m,r) congruence to rank two.\\n\\nStanding assumptions: W is irreducible and well-generated, p is positive and coprime to h, and sigma_p sends zeta_h to zeta_h^p. Choose a degree-p hsop Theta_p carrying the Galois-twisted dual reflection representation. The permutation assertion concerns this chosen standard twisted hsop quotient after forgetting the grading. The untwisted degree-p hsop in the fourth condition is an equivalent existence statement. Residues lie in0,...,h-1. The reflection-field condition and the residue condition make the same parameter range testable in different forms.\\n\\nSource: Stack–Williams, Proposition1.5, Theorem1.8 and Corollary1.9, as recalled in thm:parking-permutation-range and eq:parking-degree-test of the current paper. The earlier constructions of Armstrong–Reiner–Rhoades and the Ito–Okada classification provide the background described on an earlier slide.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nParking spaces (https://personal.utdallas.edu/~nxw170830/docs/SLC93/papers/2015%20-%20Armstrong%2C%20Reiner%2C%20Rhoades%20-%20Parking%20spaces.pdf)\\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.0 years ago; The choice of hsop in Theorem A.1 arises from the theory of rational Cherednik ... 21 Although there are subtleties to this story realized only later than [26] – see Gordon and Griﬀeth [27,\\n702\\nD. Armstrong et al. / Advances in Mathematics 269 (2015) 647–706\\nWe present here a uniform argument due to P. Etingof [18] showing the existence of an\\nhsop of degree p carrying V ∗which satisﬁes the reducedness condition of the strong or \\nintermediate versions of the Main Conjecture from Section 2.6.\\nTheorem A.1. (Etingof) There exists an hsop Θ = (θ1, . . . , θn) of degree p carrying V ∗\\nsuch that the subvariety V Θ ⊆V consists of pn distinct points.\\nThe choice of hsop in Theorem A.1 arises from the theory of rational Cherednik \\nalgebras, and to explain it requires some notation. Let ⟨−,−⟩be a W-invariant positive \\ndeﬁnite form on V , unique up to scaling. For any v ∈V , let v∨∈V ∗be the linear \\nfunctional given by ⟨−, v⟩ : V →C. Also let ∂v : C[V ] →C[V ] be the partial derivative \\noperator in the direction of v. The Dunkl operator Dv : C[V ] →C[V ] with parameter p\\nh\\nis given by\\n\\u000f\\n⟨αt, v⟩\\nDv := ∂v −p\\nh\\nα∨\\nt\\n(1 −t),\\n(A.1)\\nt∈T\\nwhere {αt : t ∈T} is the root system associated to W, normalized to satisfy ⟨αt, αt⟩ = 2\\nfor all t in T. For any t in T and f ∈C[V ], one has (1 −t).f divisible by α∨\\nt in C[V ], so \\nthe operator Dv maps polynomials to polynomials.\\nIt follows from Gordon’s work21 on the rational Cherednik algebras [26] that there \\nexists a W-equivariant injective linear map $Θ : V ∗\\u0007→C[V ]p whose image $Θ(V ∗)\\n(i) is annihilated by all Dunkl operators {Db : b ∈V }, and\\n(ii) generates an ideal I in C[V ] whose quotient C[V ]/I carries a pn-dimensional simple \\nmodule for the rational Cherednik algebra at parameter p\\nh.\\nIn particular, since the quotient C[V ]/I is ﬁnite-dimensional, for any basis x1, . . . , xn\\nof V ∗, the elements θi = $Θ(xi) for i = 1, 2, . . . , n form an hsop Θ = (θ1, . . . , θn) as in the \\nsetup (2.4) for the Main Conjecture. One then has an associated degree p polynomial \\nmap Θ : V →V characterized by a(Θ(v)) = ( $Θ(a))(v) for v ∈V , a ∈V ∗, with ﬁxed \\npoint locus\\nV Θ =\\n\\u0006\\nx ∈V : Θ(x) = x\\n\\u0007\\n=\\n\\u0006\\nx ∈V : a(x) =\\n\\u0002 $Θ(a)\\n\\u0003\\n(x) for all a ∈V ∗\\u0007\\n.\\nProof of Theorem A.1. To avoid trivialities, assume p ̸= 1 without loss of generality. \\nLet x ∈V Θ and let dΘx : V →V be the linear map given by the diﬀerential of the \\npolynomial map Θ at x. Since the generators θi−xi for the ideal (Θ−x) that cuts out V Θ\\nhave Jacobian dΘx −1V , it is enough to show that dΘx does not have the eigenvalue 1. \\n21 Although there are subtleti