ill not increase the demand for parking spaces upon private properties in the immediate vicinity of the proposed use, unless approved as shared parking in accordance with Code.\\n\\nIn all cases, at least 50 percent of the standard parking quantity shall be provided onsite, including all of the required accessible Americans with Disabilities Act parking spaces.\\n\\n**ii. Parking layout:** A site plan showing the following:\\n\\n(A) The accessible Americans with Disabilities Act parking spaces required based on the standard (non-reduced) required number of parking spaces per article V, division 5 of chapter 134 of the city Code.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nPARKING SPACES (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=2073832d3070d53275388f2aa8d6c5a8bb35ed52&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; construction of such an hsop is somewhat tricky — see Haiman [30, Proposition ... and deﬁne the algebraic W-parking space as the quotient ring ... arbitrary case considered by Gordon and Griﬀeth [28].\\nPARKING SPACES\\n9\\nis W-equivariant. In particular, the linear span Cθ1 + · · · + Cθn\\ncarries a copy of the dual3 reﬂection representation V ∗.\\nIn some cases (for example in types B/C and D, and in cases of rank ≤2) one\\ncan choose the coordinate functionals x1, . . . , xn in V ∗such that (θ1, . . . , θn) =\\n(xh+1\\n1\\n, . . . , xh+1\\nn\\n); see Sections 6 and 7 below.\\nHowever, already in type A the\\nconstruction of such an hsop is somewhat tricky — see Haiman [30, Proposition\\n2.5.4] or Chmutova and Etingof [17, §3] — and for the exceptional real reﬂection\\ngroups we know of no simple construction.\\nWhen one has such a Θ it is natural to consider the quotient ring C[V ]/(Θ).\\nFor example, this quotient occurs in the rational Cherednik theory [27, 9, 20, 21]\\nas the ﬁnite-dimensional irreducible module L(h+1)/h(1W ) corresponding to the\\ntrivial representation 1W of W, and taken at the rational parameter h+1\\nh . In the\\ncrystallographic case it is known (see [27, §5], [11, eqn. (5.5)]) that C[V ]/(Θ) is\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCITY PLAN COMMISSION (https://dallas.gov/government/meetings/DCH%20Documents/plan-commission/2-21-2019_CPC_Docket_REVISED.pdf)\\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; original building; therefore, the resulting non-reduced parking requirement is 384 parking ... PDD No. 269 also allows a 10-percent parking reduction for uses within one- ... Commerce Street, adjacent to the site in the surface parking lot, for 120 spaces and ... one additional parking space either via a remote parking agreement or through a fee in\\nPursuant to the \\nparking regulations for PDD No. 269, off street parking is required at a ratio of one \\nspace per 100 square feet of floor area and inside commercial amusement uses do not \\nrequire parking for the first 2,500 square feet of floor area in a ground level use within an \\noriginal building; therefore, the resulting non-reduced parking requirement is 384 parking \\nspaces.  PDD No. 269 also allows a 10-percent parking reduction for uses within one-\\nfourth mile of a DART light-rail station and another reduction for the 13 adjacent on-\\nstreet parking spaces. The applicant was also approved an 89-space special exception \\nto the parking requirements of PDD No. 269 by the Board of Adjustment on January 22, \\n2014.  Therefore, the total reduced parking requirement for an inside commercial \\namusement and live music venue is 241. \\nTwo remote parking agreements are filed with the Building Official: one at 2700 \\nCommerce Street, adjacent to the site in the surface parking lot, for 120 spaces and \\nanother shared parking agreement with Uplift Academy at 2625 Elm Street for an \\nadditional 120 spaces.  If the SUP renewal is approved, the applicant will need to obtain \\none additional parking space either via a remote parking agreement or through a fee in \\nlieu payment as prescribed by the PDD No. 269 off-street parking regulations. \\n \\nLandscaping: \\n \\nThe applicant’s request does not trigger any landscape requirements.  \\n \\n \\n \\n2-6--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n[Literature Review] Posh Parking Spaces (https://www.themoonlight.io/en/review/posh-parking-spaces)\\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; AI Explore how the intersection of invariant theory and Galois twists classifies posh parking spaces for complex reflection groups. ... A $W$W-stable hsop $\\\\Theta \\\\subseteq \\\\text{Sym}^p(V^*)$Θ⊆Sym p(V∗) of degree $p$p is called posh if it is a faithful $W$W-representation $U$U. ...        * Cherednik Algebra approaches: Relying on the Gordon–Griffeth construction for well-generated groups when $\\\\text{gcd}(p, h) = 1$gcd(p,h)=1.\\nSpecifically:\\n\\n$$\\\\chi_{A_\\\\Theta}(w; q) = \\\\frac{\\\\text{det}(1 - q^pw \\\\mid \\\\Theta)}{\\\\text{det}(1 - qw \\\\mid V^*)}$$χ A Θ​​(w;q)=det(1−qw∣V∗)det(1−q p w∣Θ)​Image\\n\\nThis allows for the computation of trivial and determinant multiplicities in the quotient via the identity:\\n\\n$$\\\\text{Hilb}(\\\\text{Hom}_W(\\\\text{det } \\\\Theta, A_\\\\Theta); q) = \\\\prod_{i=1}^r \\\\frac{q^{e_i(\\\\Theta)} - q^p}{1 - q^{d_i}}$$Hilb(Hom W​(det Θ,A Θ​);q)=i=1∏r​1−q d i​q e i​(Θ)−q p​Image\\n  3. Classification and Construction:\\n\\n     * Infinite Families: For $S_n$S n​ and $G(m, d, r)$G(m,d,r), the authors use coordinate-power maps (e.g., $F(x) = (x_1^p, \\\\dots, x_r^p)$F(x)=(x 1 p​,…,x r p​) for appropriate $p$p) to construct hsops and verify their poshness.\\n     * Exceptional Groups: The authors employ a combination of:\\n       * Cherednik Algebra approaches: Relying on the Gordon–Griffeth construction for well-generated groups when $\\\\text{gcd}(p, h) = 1$gcd(p,h)=1.\\n       * Seed Constructions: Defining \\\"seed\\\" maps for specific exceptional groups (like $G_4, G_6, H_3, G_{27}$G 4​,G 6​,H 3​,G 27​) which are then composed using Lemma 2.9 (composition of posh hsops) to cover all valid degrees.\\n       * Restriction Arguments: Extending covariant maps from well-generated groups to scalar overgroups or restricting from $E_8$E 8​ to $G_{31}$G 31​.\\n\\n### Main Results\\n\\n  * Classification: Theorem 1.6 provides a complete classification of all posh pairs $(p, U)$(p,U) by organizing them into specific infinite families and exceptional type tables.\\n  * Permutation Modules: The authors resolve a question by Ito and Okada, proving that the posh parking space $A_\\\\Theta$A Θ​ is a permutation module if and only if $\\\\Theta$Θ carries the untwisted representation $V^*$V∗.\\n  * Coprime Specialization: For well-generated groups with $\\\\text{gcd}(p, h) = 1$gcd(p,h)=1, they prove that the poshness of $(p, U)$(p,U) and the permutation module property are linked to the conjugacy of the $p$p-th power of a Coxeter element $c^p$c p to $c$c in $W$W.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSLC 93 - Lecture 1: Nonnesting (https://personal.utdallas.edu/~nxw170830/docs/SLC93/lecture1.html)\\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Zero-fibers of quaternionic quotient singularities--> ### <u> hsops are...complicated</u> <center><a href=\\\"./papers/2012 - Gordon, Griffeth - Catalan numbers for complex reflection groups.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/hsop 1.png\\\" width=\\\"500\\\"><br><img data-src=\\\"./img/hsop 2.png\\\" width=\\\"500\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/hsop 3.png\\\" width=\\\"500\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/hsop 4.png\\\" width=\\\"500\\\"></a> </center> ### <u> Selected References</u> <center> <a href=\\\"./papers/2012 - Gordon, Griffeth - Catalan numbers for complex reflection groups.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/Gordon, Griffeth - Title.png\\\" height=\\\"400\\\"></a> <a href=\\\"./papers/2015 - Armstrong, Reiner, Rhoades - Parking spaces\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/ARR - Title.png\\\" height=\\\"400\\\"></a> <a href=\\\"./papers/1994 - Haiman - Conjectures on the Quotient Ring by Diagonal Invariants.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/Haiman - Conjectures Title.png\\\" height=\\\"400\\\"></a> </center> <!-- 1997.\\nThe Coxeter number $h$ should be replaced by the number $g=2N/n$, where $N$ is the number of reflections in the irreducible quaternionic reflection group $W$ and $n$ is its rank. <br><br> This is true even for the well-generated complex reflection groups $G(\\\\ell,1,n)$, <center>where $h=n\\\\ell$ but $g=(n+1) \\\\ell-2$.</center><br> It turns out that for <em>all</em> irreducible complex reflection groups, there is a quotient of the diagonal coinvariant ring of dimension $(g+1)^r$ (so the quotient Iain and I were looking at is slightly wrong if one cares about the diagonal coinvariant ring); this is the main theorem of my paper \\\"The diagonal coinvariant ring of a complex reflection group.\\\" For the quaternionic case, which is still conjectural in the exceptional cases, see my preprint with Cartaya \\\"<a href=\\\"./papers/2024 - Cartaya, Griffeth - Zero Fibers of Quaternionic Quotient Singularities.pdf\\\">Zero-fibers of quaternionic quotient singularities</a>.\\\" In the appendix there is a case-free proof that $g$ is an integer. I have not begun to think about the \\\"Fuss\\\" cases.<br><br> At this moment, I do not know of a combinatorial definition that leads to the number $(g+1)^r$ that seems to be relevant in representation theory. There is some reason to be pessimistic in the quaternionic case: for the binary icosahedral group $W$, I do not believe that the zero-fiber ring carries a permutation representation of $W$ (even up to twisting by linear characters, since there aren't any non-trivial ones). So whatever combinatorics one might invent or discover, it won't be as straightforward as finding a $W$-set with the right cardinality. >$~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~$Stephen Griffeth </div> <center><a href=\\\"./papers/2012 - Gordon, Griffeth - Catalan numbers for complex reflection groups.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/Gordon, Griffeth - Title.png\\\" height=\\\"300\\\"></a>$~~~~~$<a href=\\\"./papers/2017 - Etingof - Proof of the Broué–Malle–Rouquier Conjecture.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/Etingof - Title.png\\\" height=\\\"300\\\"></a>$~~~~~$<a href=\\\"./papers/2022 - Griffeth - The diagonal coinvariant algebra of a complex reflection group.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/Griffeth - Title.png\\\" height=\\\"300\\\"></a>$~~~~~$<a href=\\\"./papers/2024 - Cartaya, Griffeth - Zero Fibers of Quaternionic Quotient Singularities.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/Cartaya, Griffeth - Title.png\\\" height=\\\"300\\\"></a></center> <!--Proof of the Broué-Malle-Rouquier conjecture The diagonal coinvariant ring of a complex reflection group. Zero-fibers of quaternionic quotient singularities--> ### <u> hsops are...complicated</u> <center><a href=\\\"./papers/2012 - Gordon, Griffeth - Catalan numbers for complex reflection groups.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/hsop 1.png\\\" width=\\\"500\\\"><br><img data-src=\\\"./img/hsop 2.png\\\" width=\\\"500\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/hsop 3.png\\\" width=\\\"500\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/hsop 4.png\\\" width=\\\"500\\\"></a> </center> ### <u> Selected References</u> <center> <a href=\\\"./papers/2012 - Gordon, Griffeth - Catalan numbers for complex reflection groups.pdf\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/Gordon, Griffeth - Title.png\\\" height=\\\"400\\\"></a> <a href=\\\"./papers/2015 - Armstrong, Reiner, Rhoades - Parking spaces\\\"><img data-src=\\\"./img/ARR - Title.png\\\" height=\\\"400\\\"></a> <a href=\\\"./papers/1994 - Haiman - Conjectures on the Quotient Ring by Diagonal Invariants.pdf\\\"><im